preConditionHook:
// THE SESSION'S FIRST-BAR TREND BAR FAILED. The bar before this one was the
// session's FIRST bar and closed as a strong trend bar; this, the session's
// SECOND bar, is a strong trend bar in the OPPOSITE direction that closes
// beyond the first bar's OPENING price. The first-bar scalp has failed and Brooks takes
// the reversal.
//
// DIRECTION, stated carefully because the merged pseudocode is written from the
// SHORT trade's point of view: the LONG side here needs the first bar to have
// been a BEAR trend bar and this bar to be a BULL trend bar closing ABOVE it,
// and the short side is its mirror. The rule's own name describes the short
// case.
//
// NOT COMPOSABLE IN ANY DIRECTION: the trade is short while the anchor bar is
// bull, so a placement's flip toggle reverses both halves at once; and the
// anchor is at lag 1 while the trigger is at lag 0.
//
// SESSION MODEL, per the standing decision: day_of(ctx.Time(1)) >
// day_of(ctx.Time(2)) -- the EasyLanguage twin's date[1] > date[2] -- says the
// bar BEFORE this one opened the session, so this is its second bar. The
// comparison is on integer day serials, not prices, so it is exact and does
// not go through el_gt; the boolean lands in a local because a rule CONDITION
// may not carry a raw relational operator.
//
// BROOKS GIVES NO NUMERIC FAILURE THRESHOLD. "The second bar closes beyond the
// first bar's open" is the mining agent's reading and is the tunable part of
// the rule; bodyFraction 0.7 carries the "strong" in both bar tests.
//
// Products, not quotients: EasyLanguage raises a runtime divide-by-zero and a
// zero-range bar is legal data.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 2.
firstBarWasOne = day_of(ctx.Time(1)) > day_of(ctx.Time(2));
